From 3374379d34a833fe49db07f00ee2f510c3bed26a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 07:15:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/18] feat(reconciler): stop a failed service's instance,
 then free its GPUs

When compensation could not stop the instance of a failed deployment,
the service went to Failed and kept its allocation until it was
deleted, so a failed service could hold its accelerators indefinitely
while its instance might still be running on them.

The round that fails such a deployment now requeues the service, and
a Failed round without a spec change stops the service's deployments
through the release provider, as Terminating does, and then releases
the allocation. A failed lookup or stop keeps the allocation and backs
off; the round writes no status. When the instance cannot be accounted
for (no deployment run, or a run that succeeded without reporting its
instance) the allocation is still kept until the service is deleted.

The controller manager uses a separate dedup operation, "reclaim", for
Failed, so the key recorded by the round that entered Failed does not
skip the release round.
